  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n06z/1am surface analysis shows a stationary front extending from\njust north of Saint Louis to near Cincinnati. Winds have shifted\nto northeasterly across much of the KILX CWA: however, drier\nair is only slowly filtering into the area...as surface\ndewpoints are still in the 70s except across the far north \naround Galesburg and Lacon where readings have dropped into the\nupper 60s.\n\nThere will be two primary forcing mechanisms in the region\ntoday...the frontal boundary to the south and a short-wave\ntrough tracking from the Dakotas into the Great Lakes. With\ncentral Illinois between these two features, it appears rain\nchances will remain low. Several CAMs indicate a few \nshowers/thunderstorms associated with the short-wave may graze \nthe northern half of the CWA along/north of I-72 this morning \nthrough early this afternoon. Meanwhile models such as the \nHRRR/RRFS have consistently shown isolated convection firing\nalong the front from central/southern Missouri into southeastern\nIllinois this afternoon. Overall areal coverage remains low on\nall models, so have only mentioned 20 PoPs in most areas. The\nmain weather story today will be the cooler conditions. A large\nnorth-south temperature gradient will exist...with highs ranging\nfrom the upper 70s far north around Galesburg to the middle 90s\nfar south near Flora and Lawrenceville.\n\nAnother short-wave trough tracking along the northern periphery\nof the prevailing upper ridge centered over the Southern Plains\nwill trigger renewed clusters of convection across Iowa into\nsouthern Wisconsin/northern Illinois late tonight into Saturday\nmorning. While most of this activity will stay north of central\nIllinois, a few showers/thunderstorms may spread as far south \nas I-72. Will need to keep an eye on the severe weather \npotential for Saturday afternoon/evening as convection tries to \nre-develop along existing outflow from the morning activity. \nWhile surface dewpoints in the 70s will support moderate \ninstability, deep layer shear will remain weak. The 00z HREF \nfocuses the highest probability (40-60% chance) of SBCAPEs \ngreater than 1000J/kg and 0-6km shear of more than 30kt along \nthe I-74 corridor. While SPC indicates a Marginal Risk (level 1 \nof 5) for severe weather across all of central/southeast \nIllinois on Saturday, think the I-74 corridor will see the \nhighest risk for a few storms with marginally severe wind gusts \nduring peak heating.\n\nWith the primary baroclinic zone wavering across the region and\nadditional short-wave troughs gradually flattening the upper\nridge, rain chances will increase further south across all of\ncentral Illinois Sunday into Monday. Instability will be less on\nSunday: however, a severe weather risk may materialize during\nthe afternoon/evening mainly south of the I-72 corridor due to \nan uptick in shear as the NAM shows 0-6km values reaching \n30-40kt. The front will sink well south of central Illinois by\nMonday, but additional short-wave energy will keep showers in\nthe forecast. After that, high pressure will build in from the\nNorthern Plains and bring cooler and less humid conditions\nthrough the end of next week. The coolest period will be Tuesday\nand Wednesday night when lows drop into the lower to middle 50s.\nThe 00z NBM even hints at cooler readings...with a 30-50% chance\nof lows below 50 degrees everywhere north of I-70.",
